
[38;5;231m⏺[39m That's the shippable shape. Now the question at the actual center of your original request — the phrase "tasks they
  care about." That's the entire notifications system in four words, and it's also the one answer that reaches back into
  the task model, so it's worth settling before I design either piece.

  The fork: caring can be [1minferred[0m from involvement, or [1mdeclared[0m explicitly, and the failure modes are opposite.
  Inferred-only means people get notified about things they've stopped caring about and have no way out, which trains
  them to ignore notifications — the one failure that kills a notification system entirely. Declared-only means nobody
  remembers to subscribe, so the notifications that matter most never arrive. Almost every tool that got this right
  (GitHub, Linear, Jira) landed on both: auto-subscribe on involvement, with an explicit unwatch and an explicit watch
  for things you're not involved in.

  Cost of that combination: it needs a real subscription record per user per task, not just a derived query over
  assignee. That's one extra table, and it's cheap now and annoying later — deriving from assignee first and adding the
  table afterward means backfilling subscriptions for every existing task and rewriting the query path.

  That combination is my recommendation. The choice is not very reversible, which is why I'm asking rather than
  assuming.
